# Source Code is Source of Truth for Parameters

**RULE: ALWAYS use TypeScript and source code as the source of truth for all parameters, types, and API surface.**

Enrichment files provide **descriptions only**—they must never invent or add parameters that do not exist in the source.

## Parameters vs Descriptions

| What | Source of truth | Enrichment role |
|------|------------------|-----------------|
| **Parameters** (names, types, required) | TypeScript / source code | None—only document what exists |
| **Descriptions** | Enrichment (when available) | Richer, curated text; fall back to JSDoc |

## Implementation

1. **Extract parameters from source** (Props interfaces, function signatures, etc.).
2. **Use enrichment for descriptions** when available: `enrichment?.parameters?.[name]?.description || prop.description`.
3. **Never add parameters from enrichment** that are not present in the source.
4. **Never override types or required status** from enrichment—those come from source.

## Example (Container Props)

```javascript
// ✅ CORRECT: TypeScript defines params; enrichment adds descriptions
const propsByName = new Map(
  containerInfo.props.map(prop => [
    prop.name,
    {
      ...prop,
      description: enrichment?.parameters?.[prop.name]?.description || prop.description
    }
  ])
);
// Do NOT add enrichment.parameters entries that aren't in propsByName

// ❌ WRONG: Adding params from enrichment that don't exist in TypeScript
if (enrichment?.parameters) {
  for (const [name, param] of Object.entries(enrichment.parameters)) {
    if (!propsByName.has(name)) {
      propsByName.set(name, { ... });  // Never do this
    }
  }
}
```

## When in Doubt

Check the actual source: `.temp-repos/{dropin-name}/src/` or `.temp-repos/StorefrontSDK/`. If a parameter is not in the Props interface or function signature, do not document it.
